---
title: API Reference
description: Complete reference for the FOSSBilling REST API.
---

{% aside type="note" %}
Building a theme or module? Use the [JavaScript wrapper](/extensions-and-development/javascript/) and Twig API helpers instead of raw browser calls when possible. They handle CSRF tokens and common response behavior for you.
{% /aside %}

FOSSBilling provides a REST API for integrating external systems and building custom functionality on top of the platform.

## API Endpoints

Three main entrypoints:

| Endpoint | Access Level | Purpose |
|----------|--------------|---------|
| `/api/admin/*` | Admin only | System management |
| `/api/client/*` | Authenticated clients | Client services |
| `/api/guest/*` | Public | No authentication needed |

## Request Format

- **Method:** Core API routes generally accept GET and POST. Guest client login/signup and staff login require POST. Use POST for mutations and requests with a JSON body or form data.
- **Format:** JSON responses. POST requests may send JSON or form data.
- **Naming:** Lowercase with underscores (`change_password`)
- **Nulls:** Blank fields are included as `null`, not omitted
- **Dates:** [ISO 8601](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601) format

## Authentication

Use HTTP Basic authentication over HTTPS. Set the username to `admin` or `client` according to the endpoint and use the corresponding API key as the password. Find your key in your staff or client profile.

Keep integration API keys on the server. Browser requests from an active client or admin session use the session cookie and a valid CSRF token.

## CSRF Tokens

CSRF protection applies to session-authenticated `client` and `admin` browser API calls. It also applies to guest client login/signup, staff login, and cart mutations. External `client` and `admin` calls authenticated with API keys do not require CSRF tokens.

For browser calls, FOSSBilling accepts the token in any of these places:

- `CSRFToken` in the JSON body
- `CSRFToken` in form data (query parameters are also accepted except for guest authentication)
- `X-CSRF-Token` request header

The token must match the server-side session token. For session-authenticated `client` and `admin` calls, a nonempty CSRF cookie must match it too. The bundled [JavaScript API wrapper](/extensions-and-development/javascript/) reads the `fossbilling_csrf` cookie and adds the token automatically to same-origin requests. Sending a cookie without a token in the request is insufficient.

### Guest Authentication and Cart Calls

Load a FOSSBilling page first to establish the session, then retain the session cookie and submit its token. These guest endpoints require a session-bound CSRF token:

- `client/login`, `client/create`, and `staff/login`: POST only, with the token in the JSON/form body or `X-CSRF-Token` header.
- `cart/add_item`, `cart/remove_item`, `cart/apply_promo`, `cart/remove_promo`, `cart/reset`, and `cart/set_currency`: GET or POST, with the token in the request. Prefer POST for mutations.

These checks apply even when `api.CSRFPrevention` is disabled. A supplied `Origin` must match the configured FOSSBilling origin, and `Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site` is rejected. Serve custom browser forms from the same origin. Server-side guest integrations must retain the session and submit its token too.

Other guest calls are not subject to this authentication/cart CSRF check. See the [migration guide](/maintenance/updating/0-7-to-0-8/#authentication-api--payment-hardening-089) for migration details.

### Manual Browser Requests

Use the [Twig form and link helpers](/extensions-and-development/javascript/#api-forms-and-links) for theme and module templates. For protected requests made directly with `fetch()`, send the current session’s CSRF token.

```javascript
const token = document.cookie.match(/fossbilling_csrf=([^;]*)/)?.[1] || '';

fetch('/api/client/profile/update', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
    'X-CSRF-Token': decodeURIComponent(token),
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ first_name: 'Jane' }),
});
```

## Example Requests

To list products, replace the hostname with your FOSSBilling host. When cURL prompts for a password, enter the API key of a staff account with permission to view products.

```bash
curl --user admin 'https://billing.example.com/api/admin/product/get_list'
```

## Response Format

**Success:**
```json
{
  "result": { ... },
  "error": null
}
```

**Error:**
```json
{
  "result": null,
  "error": {
    "message": "Error description",
    "code": 123
  }
}
```

### HTTP Status and Error Handling

Error codes in the HTTP range `400`–`599` use that HTTP status, except application codes `413`, `436`, `437`, and `498`, which use HTTP `400`. The original code stays in `error.code`. Application codes `201`–`206`, `1002`, and `1004` use HTTP `401`; `701` and `879` use `400`; and `740` uses `404`. Other application errors can still return HTTP `200`.

Read the JSON body on non-200 responses and check both the HTTP status and the `error` field. For example:

```javascript
const response = await fetch('/api/guest/currency/get_pairs', {
  headers: { Accept: 'application/json' },
});
const data = await response.json();

if (data.error) {
  throw new Error(`${data.error.message} (code ${data.error.code})`);
}
if (!response.ok) {
  throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
}
console.log(data.result);
```

For HTTP libraries that throw on non-2xx responses, read the JSON body from the exception's response. Handle non-JSON proxy or web-server failures separately.

## Common Patterns

### Endpoint Structure

```text
/api/{role}/{module}/{action}
```

Examples:
- `/api/admin/client/get_list`
- `/api/client/profile/get`
- `/api/guest/system/periods`

### Pagination

List endpoints typically accept:
- `page` — Page number (starting at 1)
- `per_page` — Items per page

Paginated results use the `FOSSBilling\Paginator` class.
